| 1 | Level 1 | Bridge: reading and writing music. *What do we need to read, sing and write music well enough to compose?* | 12 | | None | Performing (reading) and responding practiced, not claimed. Works: Scott Joplin, Maple Leaf Rag (United States, 1899, public domain score at the Library of Congress), read in short passages; a Curio Class original melody in each clef for sight-singing | Bridge days, outside the course's standards, claiming no grade 8 id (STANDARD.md 3.2). The grade 8 techniques and words Level 1 needs, as readiness: treble and bass staves and the keyboard map, note values to sixteenths and simple meter by rhythm syllables, solfege on a major scale, a chord sequence (I, IV, V), the expanded form words (introduction, transition, coda), tension and release, the describe, ask, suggest critique and the program note. Readiness look: the last ten minutes of day 1, after the hook (a hummed melody turned into notation), on the eight grade 8 rows' techniques; its results set which bridge days each student needs most. Words: staff, clef, meter, beat, solfege, chord. End: an unscored readiness record (a short sight-singing line, a rhythm dictation, a chord named), kept as the baseline, never a grade. Tools: step cards, staff paper, a keyboard map card, Curio Class's own; an interval and rhythm drill tool or its printed cards. Short path: 6 days | First because every Level 1 unit reads and writes notation, sings by solfege and uses the critique routine; many Level 1 students arrive without grade 8 music (STANDARD.md 3.2, California's framework in docs/reviews/arts-hs-music-and-levels-2026-11-16.md). Placed before unit 2 so the idea bank can be written down | None: the bridge claims no id | Hazards and controls: voice warm-up before singing, no forced singing alone (pairs or the whole class sing), headphones at a low set volume. Joplin is taught with his setting (ragtime, Sedalia and St. Louis, an African American composer of his time). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (voice and sound). |
| 2 | Level 1 | Sound for a picture or a story. *How can a few sounds stand for a mood, an image or a story?* | 36 | mu-g9-choose; mu-g9-invent | MU:Pr4.1.C.Ia; MU:Re7.1.C.Ia; MU:Cn10.0.C.Ia; MU:Cr1.1.C.Ia; MU:Cr2.1.C.Ia | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); performing (Pr4, choosing); responding (Re7); connecting (Cn10). Works: Modest Mussorgsky, Pictures at an Exhibition, Promenade and The Great Gate of Kiev (Russia, 1874, piano, public domain score), music made from paintings; Michio Miyagi, Haru no Umi (The Sea in Spring) (Japan, 1929, koto and shakuhachi), described and linked to a holding archive's page; Gabriela Lena Frank, Leyendas: An Andean Walkabout (United States, of Peruvian heritage, 2001, string quartet; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked to the composer's page | Readiness check (last ten minutes of unit 1's day 12): mu-g8-choose and mu-g8-invent, a program pick and a phrase with an introduction. MU:Pr4.1.C.Ia: pick passages in the studied simple-form works that carry a mood, image or story. MU:Re7.1.C.Ia: use the teacher's criteria (the passage carries one clear mood; it is short enough to model; its form is simple) to pick model music for the student's own piece, in the listening log. MU:Cn10.0.C.Ia: say in the log how the student's tastes, knowledge and skills shaped each pick and the piece's aim, with an invented-listener option marked the same. MU:Cr1.1.C.Ia: explain how short sound ideas (a motif, a rhythm, a register, a timbre) can stand for a mood, image or story, in the idea bank. MU:Cr2.1.C.Ia: arrange the best ideas into a first sketch of 8 to 16 bars with a one-line aim. Technique: a motif and its repetition and variation; register and tempo for mood. Words: motif, mood, image, passage, model, aim. Showing: sketches played or sung to a partner group. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Pr4.1.C.Ia, a sitting item: from two unseen public domain excerpts, mark the passage that carries the given mood and say how; MU:Re