"C:\\Windows\\System32\\WindowsPowerShell\\v1.0\\powershell.exe" -Command '$n=0; Get-Content docs/courses/music-grades-9-to-12/frame.md | ForEach-Object { $n++; "${n}: $_" }' in S:\CurioClass
 succeeded in 264ms:
1: # Course frame: Music, grades 9 to 12
2: 
3: Course `music-grades-9-to-12` (catalogue id `music-9-12`), a band course for high school run as levels, not grades: Level 1 (Proficient), Level 2 (Accomplished), Level 3 (Advanced, at honors depth) and an optional Level 4 (Advanced, deepened, a portfolio year the student leads), one level a school year (STANDARD.md 3.2). It is a composition and theory course on the national composition and theory strand (STANDARD.md 3.3). Draft frame, written 2026-11-25; the scope and sequence sets the days later, one level at a time, and each level's part of this file is that level's frame.
4: 
5: ## The course in brief
6: 
7: - **Units.** 19: five in each of Levels 1 to 3 and four in Level 4. Levels 1 to 3 share one shape, so each thread
8:   comes back one level harder in the same place: an opening unit (the Level 1 bridge, or the Level 2 and 3 review
9:   that doubles as placement), then four units that each join two progression threads, one of creating and one of
10:   responding, performing or connecting: a models and ideas unit, a form and drafting unit, a meaning and revision
11:   unit, and a rehearsal and concert unit that ends the level with the class's showing. Level 4 is one portfolio
12:   year in four units with checkpoints (APPROACH "High school levels and music").
13: - **Days.** 640: 160 a level for four levels. Each level: 156 days in its units and 4 course-level days (a
14:   mid-year check and a year-end check, each followed by one respond day). One lesson of 45 minutes a day, five a
15:   week, 32 weeks a level (STANDARD.md 3.4: a high school level is 160 course days of 45 minutes, the 120 hours of
16:   the national level descriptor). The 45 minutes hold the opener (about 5 minutes), the teaching and making, and
17:   collecting work (about 3 minutes; 5 on a recording day, when takes are saved and devices put away); a readiness
18:   or retrieval task takes ten minutes. Each unit's days include its end-of-unit assessment and, in units over 25
19:   days, its mid-unit assessment (one near the middle; two in units of 42 days or more). The bridge's 12 days are
20:   outside the course's standards (7.5 percent of the level, inside the bar's 10 percent limit); the review units'
21:   6 days are inside them. Unit counts follow the rows' load: the concert unit is longest because it owns eight
22:   ids, a rehearsal plan, a performance and a critique; the form unit is shortest in Level 1 because it owns three
23:   ids. One level is a complete course and is enough for Proficient; a school need not offer all four. These are
24:   course days: the school calendar (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md) places a level's day n on school day n. National
25:   time figure: Pending(docs/courses/arts/STANDARD.md#Music, high school): STANDARD.md 3.4 carries the music
26:   educators' association's K to 8 figure only; the high school part of the same 2020 opportunity-to-learn
27:   standards is read at source before this course page states a figure and our gap (225 minutes a week on the full
28:   path, about 112 when the short path is spread over a full year).
29: - **The short path.** Each level's scope and sequence marks an 80-day path (a semester or a rotation) that keeps
30:   every unit and every id by merging the marked days (STANDARD.md 3.2). Days on that path: Level 1, units of 6
31:   (the bridge), 17, 15, 17 and 21; Levels 2 and 3, units of 3 (the review), 18, 17, 18 and 20; Level 4, units of
32:   28, 10, 16 and 22; every level keeps its 2 check days and 2 respond days, the mid-year check on days 39 and 40
33:   (week 8) and the year-end check on days 79 and 80 (week 16, the half-year finals week). Each unit keeps, in this
34:   order and no fewer: the listening or score study that carries the unit's response to another composer's work
35:   and its connecting sentence; the technique taught with its step card; one drafting day with the critique; the
36:   revision; the showing or sitting (the check); and one respond day. Fluency days fold into the openers. A merged
37:   day never drops a critique, a revision or a respond day. The short path is 60 hours a level, half the
38:   descriptor's 120, and the course page says so.
39: - **Standard.** National Core Arts Standards, 2014, music, the composition and theory strand only, by id
40:   (`MU:Cr1.1.C.Ia` and so on), each named with its line in data/standards/ncas-performance-standards-words.txt,
41:   never quoted (STANDARD.md 2). Level 1 claims the 21 `.Ia` to `.Ic` ids, Level 2 the 21 `.II` ids, Level 3 the
42:   21 `.III` ids: 63 in all, the count in STANDARD.md 3.2, every one taught and assessed in its level. Level 4
43:   claims no new id: it applies the Level 3 ids again to new, self-directed work, and Advanced is claimed by assessed
44:   work, not by finishing Level 3 (a student who ends Level 3 at "partly" has Level 4 to reach it). The ensemble,
45:   harmonizing instrument and music technology strands are not claimed (STANDARD.md 3.3). Every level touches all
46:   four artistic processes (creating, performing, responding, connecting; bar criterion 1), and each of its four
47:   thread units touches at least two. In the Units table the C3 column is headed "Processes, anchors and works".
48:   Check set: each unit plan checks itself against the words-file lines of its ids, the nine criteria of the arts
49:   part of docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md and the lens's error list (docs/agents/lenses/arts.md); the "Check set
50:   adds" column names what that is expected to add. Values rule (DESK-ANSWERS 2026-11-12) holds.
51:   scripts/standards.py checks every id against the id file; each level's scope and sequence still states its id
52:   count (21) against STANDARD.md 3.2.
53: - **Entry and placement.** A student new to music starts at Level 1, whatever their grade; the scope and sequence
54:   names the level, never a grade. Each level requires the one before, or a placement showing scored against the
55:   previous level's end-of-level work (the review unit of Levels 2 and 3 is that showing for a new student). Mixed
56:   grades in one level are expected.
57: - **No instrument needed.** Work is shared by notation, performance or technology, and performances may be live
58:   or recorded (STANDARD.md 3.3). Days use the voice (singing, solfege, sight-singing), body and found percussion,
59:   a free keyboard on any device or a Curio Class tool, and the student's own instrument whenever they have one, as
60:   one way in. A Curio Class tool may play back a score; it never judges a composition or a performance.
61: - **Ensemble companion page.** A page inside the course, with no days and no ids: how to find a school or
62:   community ensemble, a practice log and self-check, how a composition task can be written for the student's own
63:   instrument or section, and a plain note that ensemble standards are met and assessed by that ensemble.
64: - **Base text.** None. Music 9 to 12 is Curio Class's own (docs/sources/BASE-TEXTS.md; the open-material scout,
65:   docs/reviews/arts-open-material-2026-11-16.md, found material to study, not plans to follow). Works studied are
66:   public domain scores and recordings where the holder's page says so, or, for living and recent composers, a
67:   description in our own words with a link to the composer's, publisher's or holding archive's page, never a copy
68:   (APPROACH "Rights"). Each work named below is a pick the unit plan keeps unless its rights line, checked on the
69:   item's own page, fails; a swapped work keeps the unit's ids met and the level's range. Each level's works
70:   include living composers and more than one region and period (Africa, the Americas with Native American
71:   composers in the band, Asia, Europe); the list is checked for range across the band, since open recordings lean
72:   to older European and American music. Anything students perform is public domain, openly licensed, a Curio
73:   Class original or their own. Curio Class writes every step card, score card (a short reduction of a public
74:   domain passage), listening map, draft log, rehearsal plan form, record sheet and family note.
75: - **Techniques that get harder (bar criterion 2).** Notation and reading: treble and bass staves, simple meter to
76:   sixteenths, major keys to three sharps and flats, stepwise sight-singing in solfege (Level 1); minor keys, all
77:   key signatures, compound meter, interval quality, leaps in sight-singing (Level 2); clefs beyond treble and
78:   bass, mixed and changing meter, modes and scales from outside the major-minor system, graphic notation
79:   (Level 3). Harmony: major and minor triads and I, IV, V and I (Level 1); diatonic chords by Roman numeral and the
80:   four cadences (Level 2); seventh chords, secondary dominants and modulation (Level 3). Writing: motif,
81:   repetition and variation, one-part, cyclical and binary forms (Level 1); two-voice writing, text setting,
82:   ternary and rondo (Level 2); counterpoint, theme and variations, sonata form and through-composed form, writing
83:   for the sounds a class has (Level 3). Level 4 applies them all to the student's own portfolio.
84: - **Day kinds.** As docs/GLOSSARY.md ("teaching day") and APPROACH "Kinds of day". Understanding: a technique
85:   shown in named steps with the step card, or a work studied by ear and from the score with the unit's words (the
86:   artistic process "responding" is taught here, never on a respond day). Fluency: short repeated drills with
87:   feedback, mostly the five-minute opener and two to four fluency days a unit (interval and chord naming,
88:   rhythm and melodic dictation, sight-singing a line, writing a cadence). Application: composing, drafting,
89:   rehearsing and revising with choice; the second drafting day holds the unit's one critique (describe, ask,
90:   suggest), and the next day's revision is application too. Check: a sitting with unseen items, or the showing
91:   scored from the take and the kept score. Respond: a second try at the part marked "not yet". A unit runs
92:   roughly: understanding, fluency, application, fluency, application with the critique, revision, check, respond.
93: - **Scored evidence.** Every owned id has an end-of-unit item or a look-for on the unit's guide, named id by id
94:   in the unit's "End-of-unit evidence" line. A progression row's "Assessed by" note names one form for the row,
95:   never the whole line. Items come in three kinds: a look-for on the product's guide (three to five look-fors, met,
96:   partly or not yet), scored from the kept score, draft log, plan or take after the session; a written note made on
97:   the unit's last making days, scored with the product; and sitting items on an unseen public domain or openly
98:   licensed work or a Curio Class original, played twice from the teacher's device or performed from the score
99:   card. A check day's 45 minutes: set-up 3, the student's self-check of the product on the guide 5, the sitting 35
100:   (two unseen excerpts, each heard twice), collecting 2; one adult proctors one room. A showing day (the concert
101:   units) holds only the showing: groups perform live or play their takes, and the teacher scores from the live
102:   showing and the recordings before the respond day. The product's draft, its critique with the teacher's
103:   look-for marks on the record sheet, and its revision come on earlier days counted in the unit. No score comes
104:   from the day a technique is first taught. Each unit opens with a baseline look at its main look-for during the
105:   first practice days (APPROACH "Growth"), so every student has two marks a unit. One piece a unit goes into the
106:   portfolio (a folder of scores, logs and takes) with a one-line reflection (bar criterion 8).
107: - **Readiness and retrieval.** Level 1 is open to any grade and to students with no K to 8 music (STANDARD.md
108:   3.2). The count starts from grade 9 after Music, grades 6 to 8 in grade 8, which is one grade back and would be a
109:   retrieval task; because a Level 1 student may be in grade 10, 11 or 12 or may never have taken music 6 to 8, each
110:   Level 1 unit gives a readiness check on its grade 8 row instead, with the ten-minute reteach, and the bridge
111:   (unit 1) teaches the grade 8 techniques and words first. Unit 1's readiness look takes the last ten minutes of
112:   day 1, after the hook; units 2 to 5 take theirs in the last ten minutes of the previous unit's last day or of the
113:   mid-year respond day. Levels 2, 3 and 4 assume the level before's rows, used the year before, so each unit opens
114:   with a retrieval task inside day 2's opener; day 1 stays clean for the hook and the question. A student placed
115:   into Level 2 or 3 by a placement showing meets the same retrieval tasks, each carrying its ten-minute reteach.
116:   The Seams table gives each row's last use.
117: - **Course-level checks.** Grades 9 to 12 have no stagger rule but the finals weeks (CALENDAR.md rule 4). Every
118:   level: mid-year check on days 79 and 80 (week 16), year-end check on days 159 and 160 (week 32, the finals week).
119:   The concert unit's showing (weeks 30 and 31) is its own end-of-unit assessment, and the year-end check samples
120:   the units before it.
121: - **Grade 12.** A level taken in grade 12 teaches to its last week. A senior whose school ends before week 32
122:   takes the year-end check and its respond day on their last two days, and the days that fold are the concert
123:   unit's last fluency days (up to five), never its showing, critique, revision or respond day. A student of 18 or
124:   over gives their own yes to be recorded or shown outside class and gets the family note themselves; the school's
125:   own rules still apply (AGENTS.md).
126: - **Leave-out.** A school or family may leave out a unit or a work for its community's reasons (STANDARD.md 5);
127:   each unit stands alone. Units the course page names: unit 3 (it studies Javanese gamelan, a tradition with
128:   court and ceremonial uses, by listening only) and unit 12 (it studies a concert work written about a sacred
129:   space and its history). Swapping a work keeps the unit's ids met; leaving a unit out means the course page and
130:   the unit's family note name the ids not claimed that level.
131: - **The ordinary room.** A class of 30 at desks, staff paper, pencils, the voice, body and found percussion;
132:   nothing must be bought, and every unit names a free stand-in (STANDARD.md 3.4, APPROACH "Supplies"). One student
133:   at home: the parent is the audience and the critique partner, a group piece has a solo or doubled version (the
134:   student sings one line while a device or the parent plays or claps another), and the showing is to the family or
135:   a recorded take. The teacher needs no music degree: each unit's teacher page says what to listen for, in plain
136:   words, and gives worked answers to every sitting item.
137: - **Supplies and cost.** Provisional cost per level for a class of 30, printing only (step cards, score cards,
138:   listening maps, staff paper, logs and plan forms), every item optional beside writing on plain paper: Level 1
139:   $35 (units of $10, $5, $5, $5 and $10), Level 2 $30 ($5, $5, $10, $5, $5), Level 3 $30 ($5, $5, $10, $5, $5),
140:   Level 4 $20 ($5 a unit). Planning figures, not quotes.
141: - **Devices.** One teacher device with speakers plays the works studied; with none, the teacher or the class
142:   performs the excerpt from the printed score card, and a work with no public domain score is met by its
143:   description and the score card's reduction. Students need no device: a shared device in pairs, where a school
144:   has one, may run a free keyboard or a Curio Class score tool for playback and for saving takes. No owned id needs
145:   technology: `MU:Cr3.2.C.Ia` and its later levels let a piece be shared by score or performance, and the
146:   `MU:Pr6.1` ids accept a live performance, so a paper-path student meets every id in full. Kept work stays on
147:   paper or on the student's, family's or school's own device (APPROACH "Kept work").
148: - **Paper path.** Every day runs on staff paper, the voice, body percussion and printed cards; any Curio Class
149:   tool a unit plan adds (an interval and chord drill, a score playback tool) has a printed version that does the
150:   same job, and none needs a student account.
151: - **Reading load.** Step cards and listening maps are one page; a day's reading alone is at most one page of our
152:   own text at the grade 9 level. English learners: each unit teaches its five or six words in depth, with a
153:   sound example and a frame ("The melody ___ because ___."), a student may sing, clap or play the word instead of
154:   saying it, and the check marks what the student composed or performed apart from what they wrote (APPROACH
155:   "Supports").
156: - **Safety read.** Every unit uses voice and sound, so every unit gets the fresh safety agent's read
157:   (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md) against two free guides, named here and opened by the agent: "Protect
158:   Your Hearing Every Day: Information and Recommendations for Student Musicians" and "Protect Your Vocal Health",
159:   both from the National Association of Schools of Music with the Performing Arts Medicine Association
160:   (nasm.arts-accredit.org). Rules for every unit, from APPROACH "Safe use" ("from memory" until that read checks
161:   them): headphones at a set low volume and never shared without a wipe; no loud sound at a student's ear; found
162:   percussion with no sharp edges and no glass; a voice warm-up before singing and voice rest after a long
163:   rehearsal; no forced singing alone. Each unit's last column gives its hazards and controls in the words days.csv
164:   will copy. No student sign-off form.
165: - **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md),
166:   which covers the works with the rest of the unit; no outside reader is planned (Cory, 2026-11-14). Units with a
167:   cultural or community tradition name the communities and the sources the agent studies first: unit 3 (Javanese
168:   gamelan), unit 4 (the Chickasaw Nation, Jerod Impichchaachaaha' Tate's work), unit 7 (Inuit katajjaq; the
169:   Afro-Cuban chant behind Revueltas's SensemayA�), unit 9 (Egyptian song, Umm Kulthum), unit 12 (Raven Chacon, DinAc,
170:   and the history his work addresses), unit 13 (Hindustani raga), unit 14 (Dvo�TA�k's claims about African American
171:   and Native American music; Ellington's portrait of Black American history). Every task that ties music to a
172:   student's own life (the `MU:Cn10.0` ids in units 2, 7, 12 and 18) offers an invented or public option marked the
173:   same (APPROACH "Sensitive ground"). Each section carries health's steps for "If a student tells you something"
174:   (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md) on its teacher page.
175: - **Logic model (one page in the scope and sequence).** Inputs: a 45-minute lesson five days a week, step cards,
176:   score cards, listening maps, the voice and found sounds. Activities: theory taught in steps, works studied by ear
177:   and from the score with their setting, composing with a critique and a revision, a showing each level.
178:   Outputs: a portfolio of scores, draft logs and takes, one piece a unit, and a concert each level, with two marks a
179:   unit per student on the main look-for. Outcome claimed: met marks on the level's 21 ids and growth on the
180:   look-fors. Nothing here has been tried with students, and Curio Class makes no claim that music study raises any
181:   other result (APPROACH "Sources").
