247: | 3 | Level 1 | How a piece is built. *How does a short idea grow into a whole piece in a simple form?* | 30 | mu-g9-read; mu-g9-document | MU:Pr4.2.C.Ia; MU:Re7.2.C.Ia; MU:Cr2.1.C.Ib | Responding (Re7); performing (Pr4, analysis for rehearsal); creating (Cr2). Works: a Javanese gamelan ladrang in its gong cycle (Central Java, played by a named court or community ensemble on a holding archive's page; the unit plan picks the recording and its rights line), a cyclical form; Minuet in G from the 1725 Notebook for Anna Magdalena Bach, attributed to Christian Petzold (Germany, public domain), a binary form; W. C. Handy, St. Louis Blues (United States, 1914, public domain score), a twelve-bar cyclical form | Readiness check (last ten minutes of unit 2's day 48): mu-g8-read and mu-g8-document, a sight-read line and a chord sequence notated. MU:Re7.2.C.Ia: analyze by ear how the elements and form of the three works fit their style, mood and setting, as models, on a listening map. MU:Pr4.2.C.Ia: from that analysis, write what it means for rehearsing a passage (where it repeats, where the mood turns), and plan its rehearsal. MU:Cr2.1.C.Ib: develop unit 2's sketch through three or more dated drafts in one simple form (one-part, cyclical or binary) and describe in the draft log how it grew. Technique: phrase, cadence and a binary plan (A then B, each repeated); a repeating cycle over a bass or a gong pattern; melodic and rhythmic dictation. Words: form, binary, cyclical, phrase, cadence, draft. Showing: the drafted pieces sung or played in pairs. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Re7.2.C.Ia, a sitting item: an unseen public domain work's form and one element that fits its mood, by ear; MU:Pr4.2.C.Ia, a sitting item: for the same work, one rehearsal step that follows from its form; MU:Cr2.1.C.Ib, a look-for on the kept draft log (three dated drafts, a mid-unit critique and a revision counted earlier): how the idea grew, described draft by draft. Mid-unit assessment: the second draft and its log entry, near day 64. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: rondo and ternary (Level 2). Tools: listening maps and the draft log, Curio Class's own | After unit 2 because the drafts grow from its sketch; the form analysis comes first in the unit because the drafts copy a studied form as a step, never as the end. Before unit 4 because revision to a final version needs drafts | Candidate: a binary or cyclical form from Africa or the Americas beside the three, by a living composer | Gamelan: studied by listening only, from the ensemble's or archive's own page and the community's published teaching; no student imitates court or ceremonial use, and the class composes its own cycle, never a copy (STANDARD.md 5 leave-out line). The sensitivity agent reads the archive's page first. Handy's blues are taught with their setting (the Mississippi Delta, Memphis); lyrics are not sung. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (voice and sound). |
248: | 4 | Level 1 | What the composer meant. *How do a composer's choices carry an aim, and how do we revise our own piece to carry ours?* | 36 | mu-g9-express; mu-g9-revise | MU:Pr4.3.C.Ia; MU:Re8.1.C.Ia; MU:Cr3.1.C.Ia; MU:Cr3.2.C.Ia; MU:Cr3.2.C.Ib | Responding (Re8); performing (Pr4, interpretation); creating (Cr3). Works: Florence Price, Symphony No. 1 in E minor, third movement, Juba Dance (United States, 1932), described and linked to an orchestra's or archive's page; Jerod Impichchaachaaha' Tate, Lowak Shoppala' (Fire and Light) (Chickasaw, 2006, orchestra, choirs and narrators; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked to the composer's page; Caroline Shaw, Partita for 8 Voices (United States, 2012, Pulitzer Prize in Music 2013; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked, music for voices alone | Readiness check (last ten minutes of the mid-year respond day, day 80): mu-g8-express and mu-g8-revise, an interpretation sentence and a revision against one criterion. MU:Re8.1.C.Ia: explain interpretations of the three works, citing technique, expression and genre for the composer's aim (each composer's own published words read beside the music). MU:Pr4.3.C.Ia: interpret a passage the class will sing from its elements, style and mood, tying each performing choice to the aim, on the expression map. MU:Cr3.1.C.Ia: use the teacher's criteria to judge and improve unit 3's drafts toward a final version. MU:Cr3.2.C.Ia: share the final version by score, performance or playback, with a note on how its elements serve its aim. MU:Cr3.2.C.Ib: describe how the given setting (a lunchtime showing in the room) and medium (voices and body percussion) shape the piece and its presentation. Technique: major and minor triads, I, IV, V and I, and a melody harmonized with them; dynamics and articulation marks. Words: interpretation, aim, genre, criteria, final version, medium. Showing: final versions shared at the class showing. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Re8.1.C.Ia, a sitting item: a short written interpretation of an unseen work's aim, citing one technique and its genre; MU:Pr4.3.C.Ia, a look-for on the kept expression map: performing choices tied to the aim; MU:Cr3.1.C.Ia, a look-for on the draft trail: each change tied to a teacher criterion; MU:Cr3.2.C.Ia, a look-for on the shared final version and its note: elements named as serving the aim; MU:Cr3.2.C.Ib, a written note on the last making day: how the setting and medium shaped the piece. Mid-unit assessment: the expression map, near day 98. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: self-chosen criteria and settings (Level 2). Tools: the expression map and the revision checklist, Curio Class's own | After the mid-year check because revision needs unit 3's drafts; interpretation of others' works comes first so the student asks of their own piece what they asked of the composers'. Before unit 5 because the concert performs final versions | Candidate: a European or Asian work for range beside the three American works | Tate's work tells Chickasaw history and stories: the sensitivity agent reads the composer's own pages and the Chickasaw Nation's published pages first; the unit studies it by listening and from the composer's words only, and no student imitates Chickasaw song, language or dress. Price is taught with her setting (Arkansas, Chicago, the first symphony by a Black woman composer played by a major American orchestra, 1933, as the orchestra's or archive's page states it). Hazards and controls: voice warm-up and rest; no singing alone required. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (voice and sound). |
249: | 5 | Level 1 | Our first concert. *How do we rehearse and share music so it carries its aim to an audience, and how does critique change our own work?* | 42 | mu-g9-perform; mu-g9-respond | MU:Pr5.1.C.Ia; MU:Pr5.1.C.Ib; MU:Pr5.1.C.Ic; MU:Pr6.1.C.Ia; MU:Pr6.1.C.Ib; MU:Re9.1.C.Ia; MU:Re9.1.C.Ib; MU:Cn11.0.C.Ia | Performing (Pr5, Pr6); responding (Re9); connecting (Cn11). Works: Fela Sowande, African Suite, Joyful Day (Nigeria, 1944, strings), described and linked; Ernesto Lecuona, MalagueA�a (Cuba, 1928, piano, public domain in the United States; the unit plan checks the score's page); the class's own final versions and Curio Class originals | Readiness check (last ten minutes of unit 4's day 116): mu-g8-perform and mu-g8-respond, a readiness note and a critique against own criteria. MU:Pr5.1.C.Ia: write a rehearsal plan that marks repetition and variation in the piece's form. MU:Pr5.1.C.Ib: use set criteria and feedback to name how a performance carries the elements, style and mood. MU:Pr5.1.C.Ic: find and use ways to improve technique and expression across several works (the student's own and two classmates'). MU:Pr6.1.C.Ia: share a live or recorded performance and explain how its elements carry the aim. MU:Pr6.1.C.Ib: name how a composition suits its audience or setting and what that changes next. MU:Re9.1.C.Ia: describe how well technique and expression work in a piece, using basic theory (intervals, triads, cadence, form). MU:Re9.1.C.Ib: describe how critiquing others and taking feedback can feed the student's own composing. MU:Cn11.0.C.Ia: show how a piece relates to another art, a school subject, a setting or daily life (Sowande's suite and the tunes it draws on; Lecuona's dance). Technique: sight-singing from the student's own score; balance and blend in a small group; a rehearsal loop (run, mark, fix, run). Words: rehearsal plan, repetition, variation, audience, critique, feedback. Showing: the level's concert for another class or families, with the family's yes (or the student's own at 18) for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Pr5.1.C.Ia, a look-for on the kept rehearsal plan: repetition and variation marked; MU:Pr5.1.C.Ib, a written note after the dress rehearsal: how one performance carries elements, style and mood, against the set criteria; MU:Pr5.1.C.Ic, a look-for on the rehearsal log: one technique and one expression fix tried across the works; MU:Pr6.1.C.Ia, a look-for on the showing (live or the take): the performance with its spoken or written program note on how elements carry the aim; MU:Pr6.1.C.Ib, a written note after the concert: how the piece suited its audience and one change next; MU:Re9.1.C.Ia, a sitting item on an unseen public domain performance: how well its technique and expression work, in basic theory terms; MU:Re9.1.C.Ib, a look-for on the critique trail: one feedback given, one received and what it changed; MU:Cn11.0.C.Ia, a written note: one link from a studied work to another art, subject, setting or daily life. Two mid-unit assessments: the rehearsal plan (near day 128) and the critique trail (near day 142). Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: historical setting in the plan (Level 2). Tools: the rehearsal plan form, the critique card and the program note sheet, Curio Class's own; a recording device of the school's, optional | Last in the level because the concert performs the level's final versions and ends the year; the practice-plan and describe-interpret-judge models come back from grade 8 here, where they are first needed. Its showing (weeks 30 and 31) is its own assessment, before the year-end check | Candidate: a Native American or Asian performance for the critique sitting, for range | Recording: no student is recorded without the family's yes (or their own at 18); a face or voice is never required (hands, the score or the sound alone will do); a student who does not want to be recorded is observed live and the teacher's mark stands; takes stay on the student's, family's or school's own device. No student performs alone unless they choose to: every piece has a group or doubled version. Hazards and controls: voice rest after long rehearsals; headphones at a low set volume. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (voice and sound). |
250: | 6 | Level 2 | Review and placement. *What do we bring from Level 1, and where does each of us start?* | 6 | | None | All four processes practiced as review, claiming nothing. Works: one studied Level 1 work per thread, revisited (Pictures at an Exhibition, the Bach Notebook minuet, Partita for 8 Voices) | Review days, inside the course's standards, claiming no id (STANDARD.md 3.2). The Level 1 techniques Level 2 builds on: triads and I, IV, V, binary form, the draft log, the expression map, the rehearsal plan. For a student new to the course this unit is the placement showing: a short sketch with its aim, a listening analysis and a revised passage, scored against the Level 1 end-of-level guide; a student who meets it starts Level 2, and one who does not starts Level 1 with the teacher's note (STANDARD.md 3.2). Words: review, placement, sketch, form, criteria. End: the placement record, kept unscored for returning students. Tools: the Level 1 guides and logs. Short path: 3 days | First in Level 2 because the level's retrieval tasks and its placement need one place to happen; at most 6 days (STANDARD.md 3.2) | None: the review claims no id | Hazards and controls: voice warm-up; headphones at a low set volume. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (voice and sound). |
251: | 7 | Level 2 | From a memory, a text or a sound. *How can music stand for a memory, a text or a concept?* | 36 | mu-g10-choose; mu-g10-invent | MU:Pr4.1.C.IIa; MU:Re7.1.C.IIa; MU:Cn10.0.C.IIa; MU:Cr1.1.C.IIa; MU:Cr2.1.C.IIa | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); performing (Pr4); responding (Re7); connecting (Cn10). Works: Silvestre Revueltas, SensemayA� (Mexico, 1938, orchestra, on a poem by NicolA�s GuillAcn), described and linked; Pauline Oliveros, Sonic Meditations (United States, 1971, text scores of sound events), described and linked; Tania LeA3n, Stride (Cuba and the United States, 2019, Pulitzer Prize in Music 2021; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked; Inuit katajjaq, women's vocal games of the Canadian Arctic, heard on a holding archive's or the community's own page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: Level 1's idea bank and model log (mu-g9-invent, mu-g9-choose). MU:Pr4.1.C.IIa: pick passages or movements in simple or moderately complex forms that carry meaning. MU:Re7.1.C.IIa: use given or the student's own criteria to pick model music and defend each pick in the log. MU:Cn10.0.C.IIa: show in the log how tastes, knowledge and skills shape the picks and the piece's aim, with an invented or public option. MU:Cr1.1.C.IIa: explain and show how sound ideas can stand for a memory, an image, a text or a concept. MU:Cr2.1.C.IIa: arrange many sounds into a first expressive sketch of an event, a text or a concept, with a stated aim. Technique: setting a text (stress, syllable and phrase); minor keys and compound meter; a text score of the student's own. Words: text setting, concept, sound event, movement, defend, expressive. Showing: sketches shared in groups. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Pr4.1.C.IIa, a sitting item: in an unseen movement, the passage that carries a given meaning and why; MU:Re7.1.C.IIa, a look-for on the kept log: three picks defended against stated criteria; MU:Cn10.0.C.IIa, a look-for on the same log: tastes and skills named for each pick (own or invented, marked the same); MU:Cr1.1.C.IIa, a written note: how two ideas in the bank stand for the chosen text or concept, with the ideas sung or played; MU:Cr2.1.C.IIa, a look-for on the revised sketch: many sounds arranged toward the stated aim. Mid-unit assessment: the idea bank and note, near day 184. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: abstract and extended ideas (Level 3). Tools: the log, idea bank and text-setting card, Curio Class's own | First thread unit of Level 2, the same slot as unit 2, so the imagining and choosing threads come back one level harder. Before unit 8 because its drafts grow from this sketch | Candidate: an African or Asian text setting, for range | SensemayA� sets a poem in the form of an Afro-Cuban chant for killing a snake: the unit studies the poem and the music by listening and reading only, names the chant's Afro-Cuban setting from a holding archive's or scholar's page, and no student imitates the chant. Katajjaq: listening only, from the community's own published teaching; no student imitates it. A memory is offered with a text or concept option marked the same; no student is asked to set a painful memory. Health's steps if a student tells something. Hazards and controls: voice warm-up; headphones at a low set volume. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (voice and sound). |
252: | 8 | Level 2 | Rondo, ternary and the score. *How do composers bring an idea back, and what does the score show that the ear misses?* | 36 | mu-g10-read; mu-g10-document | MU:Pr4.2.C.IIa; MU:Re7.2.C.IIa; MU:Cr2.1.C.IIb | Responding (Re7); performing (Pr4); creating (Cr2). Works: Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart, Piano Sonata K. 331, third movement, Alla Turca (Austria, 1783, public domain), a rondo written in imitation of Ottoman military band music; FrAcdAcric Chopin, Prelude Op. 28 No. 15 (Poland and France, 1839, public domain), a ternary form; Chiquinha Gonzaga, Atraente (Brazil, 1877, piano polka, public domain) | Retrieval in day 2's opener: Level 1's form analysis and draft log (mu-g9-read, mu-g9-document). MU:Re7.2.C.IIa: analyze by ear or score the elements and composing techniques of the three works and explain what they model. MU:Pr4.2.C.IIa: analyze how their elements and form fit style, function and setting (a concert piece, a salon dance), then plan the rehearsal of a passage from it. MU:Cr2.1.C.IIb: develop unit 7's sketch through dated drafts in a binary, rondo or ternary form and explain in an annotated log how and why it grew. Technique: diatonic chords by Roman numeral and the four cadences; two-voice writing; a return of the A section varied. Words: rondo, ternary, return, score, function, cadence. Showing: the drafted pieces in pairs. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Re7.2.C.IIa, a sitting item: an unseen public domain score excerpt, one technique named and what it models; MU:Pr4.2.C.IIa, a sitting item: the excerpt's function and setting and one rehearsal choice that follows; MU:Cr2.1.C.IIb, a look-for on the annotated draft log (drafts, critique and revision counted earlier): how and why the idea grew, explained. Mid-unit assessment: the second draft and its annotation, near day 220. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: complex forms (Level 3). Tools: score cards and the annotated log, Curio Class's own | After unit 7 because the drafts grow from its sketch; the same slot as unit 3. Before the mid-year check and unit 9, whose revision needs these drafts | Candidate: a rondo or ternary form from Asia or Africa, for range | Alla Turca is taught with its setting: a European picture of Ottoman music, not Ottoman music itself; the unit says so plainly. Hazards and controls: headphones at a low set volume. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (voice and sound). |
253: | 9 | Level 2 | Function and setting. *How do a work's function and setting support what it means, and why does a chosen setting suit our own piece?* | 36 | mu-g10-express; mu-g10-revise | MU:Pr4.3.C.IIa; MU:Re8.1.C.IIa; MU:Cr3.1.C.IIa; MU:Cr3.2.C.IIa; MU:Cr3.2.C.IIb | Responding (Re8); performing (Pr4); creating (Cr3). Works: Umm Kulthum, Enta Omri (Egypt, 1964, music by Mohammed Abdel Wahab), a song made for long live concerts and radio, described and linked; William Grant Still, Afro-American Symphony (United States, 1930), described and linked; Reena Esmail, TaReKiTa (United States, of Indian heritage, choir, on Hindustani rhythm syllables; the unit plan records the date and whether living, from the composer's page), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's opener: Level 1's expression map and revision with criteria (mu-g9-express, mu-g9-revise). MU:Re8.1.C.IIa: support interpretations of the three works by citing elements, techniques, genre and setting. MU:Pr4.3.C.IIa: interpret a passage from its elements, style, function and setting, supporting how each performing choice reflects intent. MU:Cr3.1.C.IIa: use given or the student's own criteria to judge and improve unit 8's drafts toward a final version. MU:Cr3.2.C.IIa: share the final version and explain how its elements and composing techniques serve its aim. MU:Cr3.2.C.IIb: explain why the chosen setting and medium (a solo or group performance, a score, playback) suit the piece and its presentation. Technique: modulation to the dominant; a melody's shape and ornament; writing for the class's voices and instruments. Words: function, setting, support, technique, medium, defend. Showing: final versions shared in the chosen setting. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Re8.1.C.IIa, a sitting item: an interpretation of an unseen work supported by one element, one technique and its setting; MU:Pr4.3.C.IIa, a look-for on the expression map: choices tied to function and setting; MU:Cr3.1.C.IIa, a look-for on the draft trail: changes tied to stated criteria; MU:Cr3.2.C.IIa, a look-for on the shared final version and note: techniques named as serving the aim; MU:Cr3.2.C.IIb, a written note: why the chosen setting and medium suit the piece. Mid-unit assessment: the expression map, near day 258. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: researched criteria and compared settings (Level 3). Tools: the expression map and revision checklist, Curio Class's own | After the mid-year check because revision needs unit 8's drafts; the same slot as unit 4. Before unit 10, whose concert performs these final versions | Candidate: a European work whose function is plain (a dance, a march), for range | Umm Kulthum: the setting (Cairo, concert radio, a whole region's listening) is taught from a holding archive's or scholar's page; no lyric is sung, and the sensitivity agent reads the source first. Still is taught with his setting (the Harlem Renaissance, the first symphony by a Black American composer played by a major American orchestra, as the archive's page states it). Hazards and controls: voice warm-up and rest. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (voice and sound). |
254: | 10 | Level 2 | Rehearse for an audience. *How do style and history shape a rehearsal, and how has feedback changed our own composing?* | 42 | mu-g10-perform; mu-g10-respond | MU:Pr5.1.C.IIa; MU:Pr5.1.C.IIb; MU:Pr5.1.C.IIc; MU:Pr6.1.C.IIa; MU:Pr6.1.C.IIb; MU:Re9.1.C.IIa; MU:Re9.1.C.IIb; MU:Cn11.0.C.IIa | Performing (Pr5, Pr6); responding (Re9); connecting (Cn11). Works: Chou Wen-chung, Landscapes (China and the United States, 1949, orchestra, on Chinese melodies and ink-painting ideas), described and linked; Shenandoah (American folk song, nineteenth century, public domain), sung in a Curio Class arrangement; the class's own final versions | Retrieval in day 2's opener: Level 1's rehearsal plan and critique (mu-g9-perform, mu-g9-respond). MU:Pr5.1.C.IIa: write rehearsal plans naming form, repetition, variation, style and the work's historical or cultural setting. MU:Pr5.1.C.IIb: use criteria and feedback to name how performances carry form, style and setting. MU:Pr5.1.C.IIc: find and use ways to improve technique and expression across varied works. MU:Pr6.1.C.IIa: share performances and explain how elements and composing techniques carry the aim. MU:Pr6.1.C.IIb: explain how a composition suits both audience and setting, and how that shapes the next piece. MU:Re9.1.C.IIa: explain how well technique and expression work, using theory and composing techniques. MU:Re9.1.C.IIb: describe how critique given and feedback received have shaped the student's own composing, pointing to the drafts. MU:Cn11.0.C.IIa: show how a piece relates to another art, a subject, a setting or daily life (Landscapes and ink painting). Technique: two-part singing in tune; phrasing and breath; a rehearsal loop with a written fix list. Words: style, historical setting, audience, varied, theory, applied. Showing: the level's concert, with the family's yes (or the student's own at 18). End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Pr5.1.C.IIa, a look-for on the rehearsal plan: form, style and setting named; MU:Pr5.1.C.IIb, a written note after the dress rehearsal against criteria; MU:Pr5.1.C.IIc, a look-for on the rehearsal log across two varied works; MU:Pr6.1.C.IIa, a look-for on the showing and its program note; MU:Pr6.1.C.IIb, a written note after the concert: audience, setting and the next piece; MU:Re9.1.C.IIa, a sitting item on an unseen public domain performance, in theory and composing-technique terms; MU:Re9.1.C.IIb, a look-for on the feedback trail: where feedback changed the drafts; MU:Cn11.0.C.IIa, a written note: one link from a studied work. Two mid-unit assessments: the rehearsal plan (near day 290) and the feedback trail (near day 304). Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: many audiences (Level 3). Tools: the rehearsal plan form, the critique card and the program note sheet, Curio Class's own | Last in Level 2, the same slot as unit 5: the concert performs the level's final versions. Its showing (weeks 30 and 31) is its own assessment, before the year-end check | Candidate: a Native American or African performance for the critique sitting | Recording as unit 5 (the family's yes or the student's own at 18; no face or voice required; takes stay on the student's, family's or school's device). No student performs alone unless they choose to. Shenandoah's setting (river and westward travel) is taught; any verse that names a people is checked by the sensitivity agent against a folk archive's page first. Hazards and controls: voice rest; headphones at a low set volume. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (voice and sound). |
