Each check day: set-up 3 minutes, the student's self-check 5, the sitting 35 (two unseen excerpts, each heard
twice from the teacher's device or performed from the score card), collecting 2. One adult proctors one room. Every
student's evidence is collected once in a window; no product is scored twice. Days above are band course days
(Level 2 starts on day 161, Level 3 on day 321, Level 4 on day 481). On the short path the same checks fall on days
39 and 40 (week 8) and 79 and 80 (week 16) of the level.

## Cross-subject

No music 9 to 12 row carries a cross_subject lean (data/progressions/arts.csv), so this course waits on no other
course and no other course waits on it. STANDARD.md 6's music and math lean (note values on fractions) is met by
grade 5. The contacts below are practiced, not owned, each with its "if later" line; all are core courses (lesson
32). A Level is open to any grade, so no contact assumes a unit of a given grade's core course.

### English language arts, grades 9 to 12

| Used here | Contact | If English language arts has not taught it yet |
|---|---|---|
| Units 4, 9 and 14 (interpretations), units 5, 10 and 15 (critiques), unit 17 (reviews) | Argument writing with claims and evidence (W.9-10.1, W.11-12.1): practiced, not owned | Each unit's step card gives the claim, evidence from the music, reasoning frame and a worked example; the music guide marks the musical evidence, not the writing standard |

### Mathematics

No unit asks for number work past grade 5 (lesson 13): durations are counted in beats and fractions of a beat;
frequency ratios and the overtone series are out.

### Social studies and science

Works are taught with their time and place on our own work cards (bar criterion 7), with no lean on a social
studies unit. A capstone (unit 18) that ties music to a science of sound or a history uses only what the student
has already studied in that course, chosen with the teacher.

## For the lead

1. **High school national time figure.** STANDARD.md 3.4 has no high school music row. Read the music educators'
   association's 2020 National Opportunity to Learn Standards, high school part, at source (nafme.org; earlier read
   "in a browser", so a browser check), and add a row headed "Music, high school" to the 3.4 table; the Pending note
   in the Days bullet then resolves. Replacement for the Days bullet's last sentences: "National time figure: <the
   figure>; this course gives 225 minutes a week (about 112 when the short path is spread over a full year)."
2. **Level 1 readiness treatment.** The count from grade 9 gives a retrieval task, but Level 1 is open to any grade
   and to students with no K to 8 music, so the frame uses a readiness check with its reteach in every Level 1
   unit. Pick: keep it; the scope gate's count may flag it, and the reason is STANDARD.md 3.2's entry rule.
3. **Check weeks.** Every level: weeks 16 and 32 (both finals weeks for 9 to 12). Left for the lead's one calendar
   pass over all after-core frames.
4. **Bridge and review units own no row.** Units 1, 6 and 11 have an empty Skills cell and `None` in Owned
   standards; frame.py accepts it. If a later gate counts units against rows, these three are bridge and review
   days by STANDARD.md 3.2.
5. **Music 6 to 8 seam.** That frame is being written beside this one; the Seams table here is taken from the grade
   8 rows' hands_on and assumes only. The seam read checks both ends against the music 6 to 8 frame.
6. **Shared doc stale (repeats the pilot's item).** docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md, arts row of "Before a scope and
   sequence", still says "Owed: the standards gate (later.csv L83); load the 63 strand ids when the high school
   music frame is written"; both are done (2026-11-21). Replacement: drop both from the owed list.
7. **Supplies ceiling (money, for Cory).** Provisional printing costs: $35 (Level 1), $30 (Levels 2 and 3), $20
   (Level 4) a class of 30, all optional beside plain paper. Pick: a ceiling of $40 a class a level.

