r's list and judge pieces or performances with them. MU:Cn11.1.7a: show how music relates to another art, a subject, a setting or daily life (Vivaldi's sonnets and his music, line by line). Technique: reading form and tempo signs on a lead sheet; syncopation clapped and played. Words: contrast, form, tempo, syncopation, setting, criteria. Showing: the two contrasting pieces sung and played for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days: day 80 the reading sitting, day 81 the listening sitting), id by id: MU:Pr4.2.7a, a kept sheet made on the last rehearsal day: both scores marked with their form and a sentence on how the elements work in each; MU:Pr4.2.7b, a written item: ten symbols on an unseen Curio Class original score, form and tempo signs among them, each named; MU:Pr4.2.7c, a written item: how culture and history lead to two different readings of one of the pieces; MU:Re7.2.7a, a written item on two unseen contrasting recordings: how elements and expression relate to how each is built; MU:Re7.2.7b, a written item: the settings of two unseen excerpts named from their cards and compared; MU:Re9.1.7a, a written item: two criteria chosen from the teacher's list, and an unseen take judged with them; MU:Cn11.1.7a, a written item: one tie between a unit work and another art, a subject, a setting or daily life. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: reading at sight, whole programs (unit 9). Tools: symbol cards, lead sheets, work cards and the listening-log page, Curio Class's own. Free path: as unit 1. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in grade 7 because the year's interpretation (unit 6) is of these two contrasting pieces, and the form signs read here are written in unit 7. Contrast is the grade 7 step in reading and responding alike. 18 days: seven ids, two techniques, two check days | Candidate: a contrasting piece by a living Native American composer, for the year's range | Mento: the agent reads a Jamaican cultural body's or a museum's page on mento first, and checks the verses kept; no accent is put on. Pipa: the piece's story (often a battle or a scene) is told in one plain sentence from a museum's or a university's published words, read first by the agent. Hazards and controls: as unit 1; syncopated bucket playing kept at the set soft stroke. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
