rformance item: both pieces performed in the group, guide look-fors on two different planned readings heard; MU:Re8.1.7a, a written item on two unseen contrasting takes: how each carries intent; MU:Pr4.1.7a, a kept program note made on the last making day: four contrasting pieces, the shared criteria and a reason for each; MU:Re7.1.7a, a written item: two of the picks compared for their tie to the student's or an invented listener's interests; MU:Cn10.1.7a, a written item: how the student's tastes, knowledge or skills shaped one of the two interpretations. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a program's intent argued with evidence (unit 10). Tools: expression maps, the program note, the listening-log page, Curio Class's own. Free path: as unit 2. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 5 because the pieces interpreted are unit 5's, already read and set in their cultures; before unit 7 because varied phrases need contrast heard and named first. The mid-year check follows (week 19) | Candidate: a contrasting take by a living woman performer from Asia or the Arab world, described and linked | "See See Rider": its words are read by the agent first, and an instrumental take may stand in. The blues: told as an African American form, from a museum's or a library's published pages, read first. Ethio-jazz: told from a museum's or an archive's page and the player's own words, read first by the agent. Interests: as unit 2. Hazards and controls: as unit 1. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
